From 593de61241fca78ea9091cd0c8c5b21cc6ec134c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dispatch Sandbox <jax1313@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 19:30:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 06/12] Money becomes whole cents, exactly, everywhere it is
 added up

Every monetary column was REAL and every monetary field a float. Decimal
appeared nowhere in the repository; 102 round() calls appeared instead, which is
what a program looks like when it patches drift at each display site while the
accumulator keeps it:

    300 fuel surcharges of 18.35  ->  5505.000000000014
    Settlement.net_payment         ->  payment_amount - factoring_fee, unrounded

The service layer already knew. get_financial_dashboard() carries a comment
recording that summing raw floats made two reports disagree by pennies on
identical data, and the workaround adopted was round-then-sum. That holds until
the rounding is the thing under audit -- and services.compute_ifta_report()
computes tax owed for a government filing.

dispatch/money.py is the type: integer cents, ROUND_HALF_UP at the single
boundary where a decimal fraction exists (Python's round() gives 0.12 for 0.125;
an invoice gives 0.13), multiplication that rounds once, and allocate() so a
split of 100.00 three ways sums back to 100.00 instead of losing a penny.

dispatch/money_schema.py is the migration, and it is DDL only. Each monetary
column gains a generated `<name>_cents` INTEGER companion computed by SQLite
from the column itself. No rows are rewritten, so it is instant and reversible;
there is no write path to forget, no trigger to mis-order, and no backfill that
can be interrupted half way. A double represents any 2-decimal amount to far
better than half a cent, so ROUND(amount * 100) recovers the cent that was
actually typed -- float drift is a property of accumulation, not of one stored
value, which is why deriving cents loses nothing and why every sum, product and
comparison now runs in cents.

The REAL columns stay, unchanged, so every existing reader is untouched. When
the last of them is gone the generated column can be materialised and the REAL
one dropped. Until then there is one value with two views, never two values.

verify_money_integrity() reports amounts that were never whole cents -- an
import, a four-decimal per-mile rate -- and does not correct them, because which
way 10.007 should go is a decision about somebody's invoice.
